    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Yearnalism is Baby Rose’s third studio album, released on July 10, 2026, via Secretly Canadian, and serves as a deeply personal exploration of desire in its many forms. Fresh off winning her first Grammy for her contribution to Leon Thomas’ MUTT, Rose returns with her unmistakable contralto voice, now steered by confidence, care, and liberating love. She defines “yearnalism” as the process and documentation of longing—desiring freedom, love, what you can’t have, and expansion—turning the album into a 12-song roadmap of desire stripped of ego and steeped in emotional honesty .\n\nThe album blends funk, soul, R&B, jazz, and gospel into a cinematic, vintage-inspired soundscape that feels both retro and contemporary. Each track dissects a different permutation of longing: lovers uniting and fracturing, dreams fading and renewing, friendships drifting, and the ache of unfulfilled connection . Rose places herself at the center of these narratives rather than observing from afar, delivering lyrics with colloquial poetry that reads like a public diary . Features from Leon Thomas on “Friends Again” and Elmiene add depth, while production by co-executive producer BIAKO shapes the album from roughly seventy sketches into a cohesive, intimate statement .\n\nYearnalism marks a sonic and emotional evolution for Rose, moving from the self-stabilizing focus of her first two records to an outward, directed yearning that embraces vulnerability without shame. Critics describe it as gloriously cinematic soul from the edge of emotional collapse, a bubble of optimism and raw complexity that rejects modern emotional avoidance in favor of unfiltered truth . With its smooth, layered neo-soul production and Rose’s versatile vocals—shifting from 70s classicism to contemporary neo-soul—the album stands as a self-assured snapshot of an artist fully trusting her signature sound and committed to loving despite fear .",
